STAGE-1 EXPEDIENT: build cmake against its own *bundled* libcurl, libexpat,
librhash and libuv instead of system copies.

This is the cut that breaks the cycle at its actual centre. `devel:libcurl` is
the load-bearing edge; the other three are dropped only because they are not
built yet.

## The cycle, precisely

The chain that was blocking `zstd` and `openssl3` is *not* the doc chain that was
blocking `gettext`. It is a tight four-node loop, all of it real linkage rather
than documentation:

    cmake  --BUILD_REQUIRES devel:libcurl-->  libcurl
    libcurl --BUILD_REQUIRES devel:libssl-->  openssl3
    openssl3 --BUILD_REQUIRES devel:libzstd--> zstd
    zstd   --BUILD_PREREQUIRES cmd:cmake -->  cmake

Evidence, from the recipes:
  - dev-build/cmake/cmake-4.1.6.recipe:50            devel:libcurl
  - dev-libs/openssl/openssl3-3.5.7.recipe:80        devel:libzstd
  - app-arch/zstd/zstd-1.5.6.recipe:102              cmd:cmake

**The status doc previously recorded `zstd` as "blocked on xz_utils
(devel:liblzma)". That was incomplete and it mattered**: liblzma was only the
*visible* dependency. `cmd:cmake` at zstd-1.5.6.recipe:102 is the one that keeps
zstd inside the loop, so satisfying liblzma alone would never have freed it.

Note this loop does not pass through netpbm or groff at all. groff/netpbm is a
separate, parallel knot (groff needs `cmd:pnmcrop`/`pnmtopng`/`pnmtops` from
netpbm plus `cmd:psselect` from psutils, and netpbm needs
libjasper/libjpeg/libpng16/libtiff/libxml2). The two knots were conflated
because both were described as "the netpbm cycle"; they are independent, and
only this one is on the path to zstd and openssl3.

## Why cutting here is the right place

cmake vendors every one of these libraries in its own tree -- `Utilities/cmcurl`,
`Utilities/cmexpat`, `Utilities/cmlibrhash`, `Utilities/cmlibuv` -- precisely so
that it can bootstrap on a machine with nothing installed. `./configure
--no-system-libs` is the upstream-supported configuration, not a hack, and cmake
does not need curl for anything on the zstd path: curl serves `file(DOWNLOAD)`,
`ExternalProject` downloads and `ctest --submit`.

The recipe **already applies exactly this technique** three lines below, with the
comment `# use the embedded copy to avoid circular deps.` for libarchive,
libcppdap and libjsoncpp. This change extends an established decision in the same
file rather than inventing one.

`--system-zlib` is deliberately kept: `zlib-1.3.2` and `zlib_devel` are built, so
there is no reason to bundle it. The build log confirms it was honoured
(`-- Using system-installed ZLIB`).

## What it costs

- **cmake carries four bundled libraries.** They will not receive security
  updates from the system packages, which for a bundled *curl* is the entry that
  matters. This package must not reach a shipping repository.
- `cmd:cmake` from this package cannot be trusted for anything that downloads
  over the network or submits to CDash. It is fine as a *build tool* for
  compiling other ports, which is its only job here.
- The `REQUIRES` lines were commented out to match, so the package does not
  declare runtime deps it no longer links.

## A second, unrelated blocker found here

The first attempt failed in dependency resolution, not in the build:

    build-prerequires "cmd:which" of package "cmake-4.1.6" could not be resolved
    Error: unable to resolve prerequired packages for build for cmake-4.1.6

Nothing in the image provides `cmd:which` -- Haiku has no `which` binary and no
installed package declares it. The four cut edges resolved silently, so this was
the *only* thing left. It is **not** an expedient and needed no cut: the provider
is `sys-apps/which/which-2.21`, a ~15 KB GNU package whose entire
BUILD_PREREQUIRES is `cmd:awk cmd:gcc cmd:grep cmd:make cmd:sed`. It was built in
under a minute (`which-2.21-6-arm64.hpkg`, plus debuginfo) and cmake then
resolved. Worth remembering as a class: a missing `cmd:` provider can look like
part of a cycle when it is just an unbuilt leaf.

## Retire when

curl, expat, rhash and libuv exist as native arm64 packages -- which becomes
possible the moment this cmake exists, because that is what was blocking them.
Restore the four `--system-*` flags plus `--bootstrap-system-libuv` and
`--bootstrap-system-librhash`, uncomment the four `devel:` and four `lib:` lines,
and rebuild. Then rebuild anything compiled by the stage-1 cmake is *not*
required -- cmake is a build tool, its bundled libs do not leak into the packages
it builds -- but cmake itself must be replaced.

Applies to: input-source-packages/develop/sources/cmake-4.1.6-1/cmake-4.1.6.recipe

